 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Foreign relations",
  "title": "Three relationships in one week: Poland builds ties, India waits, and Warsaw and Delhi move slowly",
  "body": "**US–Poland: defence ties deepen while political messages diverge.** This week brought solid defence deliverables. Deputy minister Cezary Tomczyk signed a [defence R&D agreement](https://notesfrompoland.com/2026/11/24/poland-and-us-sign-defence-technology-cooperation-agreement/) at the Pentagon after 17 years of talks, on Nov 19 the State Department told him it would support Polish efforts on the base and Tomahawk purchase, and two more F-35s reached Łask. The headline item has slipped, though. Deputy minister Zalewski now expects the White House decision on permanently stationing US troops around the turn of the year, not in early December. The two governments have also differed openly. Tusk called the Nov 20 Kh-101 incursion a provocation and said he does not always share the US reading. Klich briefed the NSC, but no US statement on the incursion followed. Trump told Putin to stop 'aggression' in Europe without naming Poland.\n\n**US–India: the relationship turns on the trade deal.** USTR's proposal of 25% on Indian goods, or 17% with a deal, has made the deal the centre of the relationship. On Nov 27 Goyal said it was 'close to culmination', and exporters want it done before the January hearings. The defence and Quad agenda has been quiet since Manila. Friction points remain: Nikhil Gupta's sentencing, summonses for NSA Doval in the Pannun civil suit, 1,700 deportations so far this year, and H-1B pressures such as IBM's relocate-or-leave choice. **Analysis:** the 17% offer gives both sides a reason to announce something at the G20 in Florida (Dec 14–15), but no bilateral meeting has been confirmed.\n\n**Poland–India: plenty announced, little delivered.** Tusk's October visit promised a Defence Industry Dialogue and a classified-information agreement 'soon'. The agreement is still unsigned, and its watch date is Nov 30. WB Group's planned drone investment has not been confirmed. **Analysis:** without that agreement, defence co-production cannot get past talks. This week's visible link was cultural: a large Polish programme at IFFI in Goa.",
